---
import Page from '../components/Page.astro';
import { OPERATOR } from '../data/site';
---
<Page title="Disclosure" path="/disclosure/" description="How Navigator Coast is funded, how it relates to Tourground and the operators in its directory, and how paid content is labelled."
  sources={[
    { title: 'Tourground: plans and FAQ', url: 'https://tourground.com/' },
    { title: 'Google Search Central: Qualify your outbound links', url: 'https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/qualify-outbound-links' },
  ]}>
  <h2>Publisher</h2>
  <p>Navigator Coast is published by Tourground. Tourground works with travel companies and earns money from paid plans sold to them. Some travel brands are owned by Tourground's founders.</p>
  <h2>The “Plan this trip” box</h2>
  <p>Every post ends with a “Plan this trip” box linking to a local operator listed in the Tourground directory. At launch, the Portugal operator listed there is <a href={OPERATOR.url}>{OPERATOR.name}</a>, which plans stays of one to twelve months. The box is part of how Tourground runs this site; it is not a review, a ranking or a guarantee. Check any company yourself before you pay, including its registration with Turismo de Portugal.</p>
  <h2>Editorial independence</h2>
  <p>Stories are written to be useful to travellers. Some stories name hotels, restaurants, cafés and wineries. We describe them factually, from their own websites or official tourism sources, and link to them. No company pays to be mentioned, we have no commercial relationship with the places we name, and a mention is not a review or an endorsement.</p>
  <h2>Advertising</h2>
  <p>If we ever publish paid content, it will be labelled “Advertisement feature” at the top of the page and on every card that links to it, shown on a separate coloured background, and its links will carry <code>rel="sponsored"</code>.</p>
  <h2>Links</h2>
  <p>Links to official sources inside our stories are citations, not endorsements.</p>
</Page>
